<ArticleLayout
  title="How to Track Stress with a Wearable: HRV, Sleep & Limits"
  description="Learn what wearable stress scores use, why apps can disagree, and how to review HRV and sleep without mistaking a wellness score for cortisol testing."
  updated="2026-11-12"
>
<InShort points={[
  'A wearable records <strong>body signals</strong>. It does not measure stress or cortisol.',
  'Different apps use <strong>different formulas</strong>, so their scores can disagree.',
  'Start with the <strong>raw readings and their times</strong>, then look at scores.',
]} />

<h2>Can a wearable measure stress?</h2>
<p><strong>No, not directly.</strong> A wearable records signals that can feed into a stress estimate. It does not measure your mental stress or how much cortisol you have.</p>
<p>Heart rate, heart rate variability (HRV) and sleep each show a different part of how your body is doing. Apps combine some of these with their own assumptions.</p>

<h2>What should I look at first?</h2>
<p><strong>Start with the records underneath the score.</strong> Each one answers a useful question, but each has limits.</p>
<div class="not-prose my-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] p-4">
    <p class="font-extrabold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">HRV</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink)]"><strong>Ask:</strong> how does this reading compare with my history, using the same way of measuring?</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)]"><strong>Can't tell you:</strong> how much cortisol you have, or why it changed.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] p-4">
    <p class="font-extrabold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">Heart rate</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink)]"><strong>Ask:</strong> was I resting, moving or exercising when this was recorded?</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)]"><strong>Can't tell you:</strong> whether mental stress made your pulse faster.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] p-4">
    <p class="font-extrabold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">Sleep</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink)]"><strong>Ask:</strong> how much sleep was recorded, and is the record complete?</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)]"><strong>Can't tell you:</strong> why you woke up, or whether you have a hormone disorder.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] p-4">
    <p class="font-extrabold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">Wrist temperature</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink)]"><strong>Ask:</strong> does my device support it, and what change was recorded?</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)]"><strong>Can't tell you:</strong> your cortisol or your core body temperature directly.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>For Apple Watch, see Apple's pages on <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/120277">heart measurements</a>, <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108906">sleep</a> and <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/102674">wrist temperature</a>. What's available depends on your device, settings and how much you wear it.</p>

<ZenPicture src="/app-ui/start/zen_watch.webp" caption="Check the readings first. The score comes second." />

<h2>Why do two stress apps disagree?</h2>
<p><strong>They can use different records, time windows, baselines and formulas.</strong></p>
<p>Even "HRV" can mean different things. One measure, SDNN, is not the same as another, RMSSD, so compare like with like. A 60 in one app is not the same as a 60 in another.</p>
<p>Check when the latest reading was taken, what's missing, and how the app explains the number.</p>
<p>An app updating more often doesn't mean the sensor took a new reading. Apple Watch takes background readings only now and then.</p>

<h2>How does Cortisol+ use the data?</h2>
<p><strong>Cortisol+ combines the HRV, sleep, heart rate and activity data it can get into a 0–100 wellness score.</strong></p>
<p>Its <a href="/how-it-works/">published methodology</a> explains the weights, how it learns your baseline, and the limits when data is missing. The score does not model a proven cortisol level or a clinically tested hormone curve.</p>
<p>Use it to explore your patterns and ask better questions about your routine.</p>
<p>Don't use it to diagnose burnout, overtraining or a hormone disorder, or to check whether a supplement worked.</p>

